window.BOARD = {"rendered":"2026-11-28T12:35:46Z","prime_owner":"neve","types":["sketch","bug","enhancement","feature","research"],"areas":["board","config","ide","reed","fabric","selfreport","shuttle","burler","webster","stencil","loom","quarry","batten","shed","orch","creel","claude","codex","report","workspaces","deploy","dotnet","gate","testkit","pattern","shortcut","dobby","tender","hibernate","drift","darn","arbiter","landing","cleanup","termwindow","logger","git","cwd","friction","lenses","reconcile","preflight","parsers","process","fsutil","cli","pawl","lint","profiles","docs","heddle","tools","slack","weaver","testmap","desktop"],"sections":["Running","Layer A","Layer B","Layer C","Layer D","Layer E","Layer F","Draft","Research","Enhancements","Features"],"projects":[{"handle":"P0","slug":"root","title":"Root: entries under no project","brief":"","labels":[],"parent":"","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P1","slug":"test-speed","title":"Faster gates: measure where test time goes, then cut or restructure the heavy packages","brief":"","labels":["enhancement","gate","testkit"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-test-speed.md","done":false},{"handle":"P2","slug":"borrowed-orchestrator-ideas","title":"Ideas borrowed from comparable agent orchestrators","brief":"","labels":["feature","burler","shed","shuttle","orch","report"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-borrowed-orchestrator-ideas.md","done":false},{"handle":"P3","slug":"self-healing-autonomy","title":"Autonomy settles known run stops itself, so lym runs overnight and only severe halts reach the operator on Slack","brief":"","labels":["feature","shed","dobby","config","cwd","board","orch","slack","loom","batten","tender","shuttle","webster","arbiter","stencil","creel","heddle"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-self-healing-autonomy.md","done":false},{"handle":"P4","slug":"fabric-core","title":"Fabric split into a git-free core, a layout package and a thin git engine","brief":"","labels":["enhancement","fabric","testkit","pattern","cwd","loom","batten","landing","gate","orch","board","webster","drift","report","shed","stencil","heddle","ide","reconcile","docs"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-fabric-core.md","done":false},{"handle":"P5","slug":"plan-ahead-chains","title":"Tapestry chains plan the next task while the one before still builds","brief":"","labels":["feature","shed","loom","batten"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-plan-ahead-chains.md","done":false},{"handle":"P6","slug":"intake-motifs","title":"Intake motifs: run watch, landed records, closed area","brief":"","labels":["enhancement"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"","done":false},{"handle":"P7","slug":"closed-area","title":"A closed area per hub: Landlock and a seccomp connect supervisor","brief":"","labels":["feature","creel","cwd","reed","shuttle","claude","codex","dobby","slack","process","git","cli","docs","pattern","termwindow","hibernate","batten","ide","deploy"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-closed-area.md","done":false},{"handle":"P8","slug":"implementer-gate-time","title":"Webster and Weaver spend less of their row in gate runs","brief":"","labels":["enhancement","gate","webster","weaver"],"parent":"P0","design_file":"design-implementer-gate-time.md","done":false}],"entries":[{"handle":"T1","slug":"weaver-bench-fixes","title":"Weaver gets a batch timeout of its own, names deviations in its summary, records gate spans and is tested on the paths its bench missed","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["weaver","shed","shuttle"],"status":"running · Code-Burler-Fix","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Why\n\nThe Weaver bench of 2026-11-27 (`docs/research/weaver/bench-2026-11-27/report.md`) found Weaver can replace Webster as tapestry's implementer,\nbut named four changes to make first (operator, 2026-11-28: fix them, then bench again).\n\n## Changes\n\n1. A Weaver attempt gets a timeout of its own, sized to a batch.\n   Today an attempt runs under `shuttle.run_timeout_min` (30 minutes), which its gate waits and gate fixers count against;\n   the bench's first calibration batch hit it with three of four cards committed, and the retry climbed to Opus.\n2. Weaver's `summary.md` names each batch's deviations and departures, as Webster's summary does.\n   Today Weaver records the deviations only in `state.json`, and its summary lists attempt counts.\n3. The Weaver row records `gate_spans` for the plan-level verify, as the Webster rows do.\n4. Tests pin the paths the bench did not reach: a lost strand, a resume, a failed plan-level verify and the ladder's third rung.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T11:36:30Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:36:30Z","design_file":"design-weaver-bench-fixes.md","decision_file":"","revision":"1ad1f42f094ec44eb945ef816342a147b88c9e0abf1f2a303dc1dac68c0878a4"},{"handle":"T2","slug":"kde-window-placement-build","title":"Place lym's Konsole windows on KDE Plasma 6 desktops and tiling zones, remembered per hub","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["termwindow","desktop","reed","dobby","cwd","fabric","gate","pattern","docs"],"status":"running · Webster","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `kde-window-placement`.\n\nTakes: termwindow's Launcher.OpenAttach and Resolve, desktophook's AfterWindow runner and config, dobbyengine's per-task lookup over hubgeom.TaskSettingChain, hubgeom.ParentOf, state.UpdateJSON, lock.AcquireWriteLock, standalonestate.UserStateDir, selfexec.LymBinary, runproc.Register, lymtemp, and the layout as fabric-core-foundation and whole-repo-guards-off-gates land it; the KWin facts Open risks lists, probed as the run's first step, each with a KWin script run through `qdbus6` and its console.log lines read from the journal: the read-only probes run first, the moving probes only once the operator runs or approves them, and a probe that contradicts a listed fact stops the run for the parent, with the probe's command and output, before any code rests on it\nDelivers: D1's pre-launch checks and --separate Konsole launch returning its pid; internal/kwinplace (new) with the script runner, script lock, query cache, kscreen reader, pure planner, places file and placement child; the hidden placement verb that lym reed window and lym machine start hand each window to; desktop.window_placement, desktop.tiling.min_tile_width and min_tile_height, and beam.window_placement; a cancellable window_command runner; the real-desktop contract test in a manual tier\nExports: kwinplace's placement entry and planner, termwindow's pid-returning launch and pre-launch check, dobbyengine.LookupWindowPlacement, the three desktop keys\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/kwinplace (about 2600 changed lines)\n- internal/termwindow (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/desktophook (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/beamsetting (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/fabricengine (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/lymdirs (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/gateslot (about 20 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 30 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 10 changed lines)\n- docs (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-27T14:33:20Z","revised":"2026-11-27T14:33:20Z","design_file":"design-kde-window-placement-build.md","decision_file":"decision-kde-window-placement-build.md","revision":"454c2dfb6f2ba3dc345722a705aee7d37936d60067113949a3fd46eb9259010e"},{"handle":"T3","slug":"config-key-comment-sweep","title":"Name config keys as \u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e in Go comments, and guard string literals for bare keys","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["docs"],"status":"landing · Finalize","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"After `config-keys-nested` nests the config keys by area, about 525 Go comment lines in about 199 files, plus single-word keys on top, still name a template key bare.\nPATTERN-config-key-names asks that such a comment say `\u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e`.\n\nSweep every Go comment and package doc that names a config key to its nested `\u003cmodule\u003e.\u003cpath\u003e` form.\nA comment-only change, no behaviour or user-visible text.\nDeferred from T3 code review round 1, finding F1, to keep that run short.\n\n\n## From issue #265\n\nImported from [issue #265](https://github.com/Knatte18/loomyard/issues/265).\n\n## What happened\n\nA code review finding asked for a repository-wide sweep of config key names written without their module, and no tool finds them.\nA grep over the underscored template keys was the only practical method.\nIt missed single-word keys such as `engine` and `scoper`, and any key that ended a string literal, so messages were reported done while several were not.\nSelf-check forks caught the misses.\n\nAn earlier implementer had also found bare key names in messages and help texts outside its card's packages:\n`gatecli`, `batcher`, `battencli`, `beamcli`, `webstercli`, `hibernatecli`, `reportcli`, `preflightshed`,\n`weaverengine` (`ResolveLadder` and `settingRefusal`), `sheddriver/tackler.go`, `shedarbiter`, `burlerengine/fix.go` and `loomshed/plancontext.go`.\nSome of these may have been fixed since.\n\n## Why it is abnormal\n\nA message or help text naming a key without its module can regress at any time, and nothing detects it mechanically.\n\n## What to do\n\nAdd a guard test that scans Go string literals for a config template's key names written without their module.\n\nProvenance: task `config-keys-nested`, notes `burler-code-r1.md`, `11-module-key-messages.md`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T13:52:00Z","revised":"2026-11-26T20:19:01Z","design_file":"design-config-key-comment-sweep.md","decision_file":"","revision":"453d3a396dde0e27716bff4acaab72dca2032ffc2fc016c27edd0128dbe07dbd"},{"handle":"T5","slug":"pr-session-mail-stand-ins","title":"PR-Review session tests run over a real creel daemon and the recorded tmux kit, which learns to replay pastes","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["testkit","landing","reed","creel"],"status":"landing · Finalize","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"Move the MailAgent rows to a tier1 test over a real creel daemon and drop the envkit stand-ins","body":"## Seen\n\nThe run that gave testkit a recorded tmux stand-in under reedengine left two stand-ins out of its scope by the parent's decision:\nthe `MailAgent` closures in the PR-Review session tests, one returning a made-up \"no such strand\" error,\nand the session stand-ins in `envkit`.\n`MailAgent` posts through creelcli's `GoClient`, which talks to a creel daemon it spawns, so no real creel path runs at tier0.\n\nThe landed recorded tmux kit (`internal/testkit/tmuxrec`) neither records nor matches a call's stdin.\nEach paste names a fresh random buffer in its arguments, so a replayed paste matches no recorded one and fails as unanswered.\nNo current recording holds a `load-buffer` call, so a scenario that sends text to a pane cannot use the kit.\n\n## Change\n\n- Move the `MailAgent` rows to a tier1 loomcli test over a real creel daemon.\n- Teach `tmuxrec` to record and match a call's stdin, and to match a paste's buffer name by pattern rather than literally, with a recording that pastes and a replay test over it.\n- Move `envkit`'s session stand-ins onto the recorded tmux kit, using paste replay where they send text.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-27T19:02:48Z","revised":"2026-11-28T08:45:18Z","design_file":"design-pr-session-mail-stand-ins.md","decision_file":"","revision":"53adfe54ed0dfad466cc93bd05704b96bbdc9625cab367f9e9e4e21ec914fbdc"},{"handle":"T6","slug":"recipe-build-from-sketch","title":"Jacquard, a recipe that runs the tapestry rows after Discussion and refuses an entry with no stored decision record","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["shed","loom","batten","board"],"status":"running · Discussion-Burler-Review","project":"P5","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nThe operator now seldom runs a full tapestry without a motif first.\nA task raddle files from a Sketch already carries `discussed`, so its tapestry run skips Discussion;\nwhich tapestry run does Discussion and which does not is read from a flag, not from the recipe named.\n\n## Idea\n\nA new recipe, `jacquard`, holds the tapestry rows after Discussion: Plan, Plan Perch, Webster, review and landing.\nIt runs only on an entry with a stored decision record, a task filed from a Sketch or a discussed entry, and refuses any other at start.\nRaddle names jacquard for the parts it files from a Sketch where it names tapestry today.\n\n## Tapestry stays (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nTapestry stays a recipe, and it is made of motif followed by jacquard: one source for each part, never a copy of jacquard's rows in tapestry.\nThe operator can say \"run jacquard\" with no doubt about whether Discussion runs, and \"run tapestry\" for both.\n\n## Name (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nThe recipe is `jacquard` in code, config, verbs and records.\nThe operator calls it \"jacq\" in chat and text; the task adds `jacquard` to lym's glossary (`internal/heddleengine/glossary.md`)\nand \"jacq\" to the nicknames in the repository's instructions, beside \"Sketch\" and \"Merriam\".\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"motif","created":"2026-11-28T11:29:56Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:56:08Z","design_file":"design-recipe-build-from-sketch.md","decision_file":"","revision":"e3efefcdca7a02e64ff569db96ea29c4b20b58a54173a9216be6e254165e849d"},{"handle":"T8","slug":"landing-queue","title":"A landing queue gates several ready runs as one batch and lands them together","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["landing","gate","batten"],"status":"running · Discussion-Burler-Review","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-27)\n\nEach landing ran its own landing gate under the parent lock, one after the other, so with many runs ready the gates queued in series.\n\n## Seen again (2026-11-28)\n\nWith five runs ready at Finalize, each landing made every waiting run's lock-free gate stale:\nthe parent moved by real code, so each run in turn merged the new tip and gated tiers 0 to 2 again under the parent lock, 10 to 15 minutes each,\nwhile the waiting runs' own lock-free gates competed with the holder for the same gate slots.\nThe operator released this entry ahead of the measurement of `landing-lock-only-for-land` on that ground (operator, 2026-11-28).\n\n## Since (`d87a9ce24`, `landing-lock-only-for-land`)\n\n`Finalize` now merges the parent's tip and gates the merged tree with no parent lock held, and takes the lock only to land.\nReady runs therefore gate side by side.\nA run whose parent moved during its gate merges the new tip and gates again,\nand after `landing.finalize_lost_races` lost races (default 2) it merges and gates under the lock.\nEach ready run still pays for its own gate, plus one for each race it loses.\n\nSince `5c985d926` (`finalize-owed-tips-gate`), this also holds when the parent is ahead of origin only by owed landing tips,\nexcept for a landing that needs GitHub only because of GitHub's repository rules.\nBefore, every landing on such a parent lost all its races and gated under the lock.\n\n## Idea\n\nA landing queue in the manner of GitHub's merge queue, bors or Zuul:\n\n- A run ready to land joins a queue for its parent branch instead of gating and taking the parent lock itself.\n- The queue takes the waiting runs, say A, B and C, builds the tree parent + A + B + C and runs the landing gate once on it.\n- A pass lands all of them together.\n- A failure is bisected (parent + A + B, and so on): the runs that pass land, and the culprit goes back to its own run's fixer.\n- A later step may gate the prefixes in parallel (parent + A, parent + A + B, parent + A + B + C) so a failure costs little time.\n\nNo drift: the batch is gated exactly as it lands.\nIt saves the most gate time when many runs land often, and it costs the most to build:\na queue service, batch merging, bisection on failure and fixer handling for the culprit.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"motif","created":"2026-11-27T07:03:20Z","revised":"2026-11-28T12:04:11Z","design_file":"design-landing-queue.md","decision_file":"","revision":"cff9cbc8631d753eca79faa36ccaef5debcd8ddf2517ac9115b9c68d7e400530"},{"handle":"T9","slug":"implementer-gate-time-measure","title":"Measure where Webster and Weaver rows spend their gate time since the card gate took in dependent packages","kind":"task","types":["research"],"areas":["gate","webster","weaver"],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P8","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Why\n\n`docs/research/webster/webster-time-2026-11-08.md` named five levers worth 15 to 23 min of an 86 min Webster row, the largest the guard step in every card gate.\nThe Weaver bench of 2026-11-27 (`docs/research/weaver/bench-2026-11-27/report.md`) then found gate tier time per row 2.2 times its recorded figure,\nthe same day `a69580709` made the card gate also run the packages that depend on a card's packages.\nNeither cause is measured, so the cuts would rest on guesses.\n\n## What to measure\n\nRead recorded data only: gate run records under the Bolt checkout's `.lym/gate/runs`, implementer state and row history, and the archived runs' records.\nStart no run, agent, bench or gate call.\n\nTake every Webster and Weaver implementer row that ended between 2026-11-20 and the task's start, split at `a69580709`'s landing.\nFor each, split the row's time into agent work, card gates, batch gates, plan-level verify, gate fixers and gate-slot waits.\nWithin the card gates, give the time of each step of the tier commands, the guard step and the dependent-package scope included.\n\n## Report\n\nA report under `docs/research/webster/` with those tables, before and after `a69580709`,\nand for each of the 2026-11-08 levers whether it still holds and what it would save now.\nAdd any new lever the records show, and rank all of them by minutes saved per row.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T11:59:51Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:59:51Z","design_file":"design-implementer-gate-time-measure.md","decision_file":"","revision":"dad5c273ad5231a646a152878cd605d83b20e173fcf0187ff39ccfef048ccff7"},{"handle":"T23","slug":"fabric-core-push-origin","title":"Fabric core: push classification, retries, origin reconcile and failed-push records move to the core","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","board","pattern"],"status":"running · Plan-Write","project":"P4","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the foundation part's layout, core types, reader and executor loop\nDelivers: the rules of pushclassified, pushanchored, coalesce, failedpush, origin, originreads, originreconcile, originreconcileended, originreconcilemoves, pendingflags, pusherreads, pushlockholder and remoteonly in internal/fabriccore as phase and action-list rules over made-up snapshots, with the retry loop as wait actions and OriginSeams' teardown as a seam-call action; the engine running them, with rejectionRetryKind's error-to-class mapping and the live-pair probe before an origin move kept in the engine; these files' shedrun and shedengine imports cut: run paths, the prime run lock and the status codec reach fabric through required seams, the status readers return bytes their callers decode; D13's seam installer in internal/fabriccli, its call from cmd/lym/main.go and internal/hubforge's one-time call, each required seam's setter with its restore, the unset-seam refusal row and engine test, and shed's adapter with its test in the shed module; the D12 moves of this concern's boardkit and boardengine tests, such as pushclasses, pushanchored and originreconcile*, to internal/boardengine; PATTERN-fabric-boundary's background recorder clause restated as D4 words it, since this part's executor first records entries. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: the required-seam installer and setters later parts add seams to; the push and origin core rules ReconcileOrigin callers reach through the unchanged public verbs\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 4100 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2500 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccli (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/hubforge (about 20 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 400 changed lines)\n- internal/boardengine (about 1200 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-28T10:41:16Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-push-origin.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-push-origin.md","revision":"7e2cec9c1070023ccd62fc048de04748e0780770396e815372f5deb2c32b44c6"},{"handle":"T29","slug":"beam-orch-reads-down","title":"A running beam orch reads as down, and a strand missing from reed state is not covered","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["orch","reed","cwd"],"status":"landing · Finalize","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nIssue #232 reported a running beam orch that lym read as down.\nThe landing of `beam-member-parent` (`b439f157e`) made the parent check admit every orch on the chain whether or not it reads as live,\nbut its commit says the cause of the down reading is not known,\nand that the change does not cover the case where the orch's strand is also missing from reed's state.\n\n## Idea\n\nFind why a live beam orch reads as down: the liveness read, the strand's record in reed's state, or both.\nFix the read so a live orch reads as live, and make an orch whose strand is missing from reed's state read as down with that cause named.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T09:48:04Z","revised":"2026-11-28T09:48:04Z","design_file":"design-beam-orch-reads-down.md","decision_file":"","revision":"a5afada30bef8981dcc268609cfdbdda2c6cbb992a381ee29d828edb32debea1"},{"handle":"T30","slug":"clone-reset-keeps-unpushed","title":"lym fabric clone --reset removes unpushed records, board commits and uncommitted Bolt changes","kind":"task","types":["bug"],"areas":["fabric"],"status":"running · Darn-Write","project":"P0","section":"Running","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nThe landing of `hardener-study-faults` (`7cb004818`) says `lym fabric clone --reset` still removes unpushed records and board commits,\nuncommitted Bolt changes and ignored files, and leaves open whether that is acceptable.\n\n## Operator decision (2026-11-28)\n\nIt is not acceptable: `--reset` refuses when it would lose work, and names what would be lost.\n\n## Idea\n\nBefore `--reset` removes anything, check for unpushed records and board commits, uncommitted Bolt changes and ignored files that hold work.\nWhen any is found, refuse with the list and the way forward: push or commit them first, or pass an explicit flag that accepts the loss.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T09:48:05Z","revised":"2026-11-28T09:48:05Z","design_file":"design-clone-reset-keeps-unpushed.md","decision_file":"","revision":"56af52cfb99d786a1e347514b8733af25ee2caa052f4813b866e775d93023159"},{"handle":"T4","slug":"weaver-bench-rerun","title":"Bench Weaver against Webster again on the same three landed plans, both run side by side the same day","kind":"task","types":["research"],"areas":["weaver","webster"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T1"],"brief":"","body":"## Why\n\nThe bench of 2026-11-27 (`docs/research/weaver/bench-2026-11-27/report.md`) had only one plan where Weaver and Webster ran the same day,\nso its quality and cost comparison is thin.\nIt is run again after Weaver's four named changes have landed (operator, 2026-11-28).\n\n## Runs\n\nExactly six implementer runs and six reviews:\n\n- The same three plans as the first bench: `board-viewer-server`, `launch-manifest-parity` and `gate-test-map-mode`, from the same base commits.\n- Each plan once with Weaver on its default ladder and once with Webster on its default models, the two started together, the same day.\n- One Burler round per result, as in the first bench: a `sonnet[medium]` reviewer with the code lens fan and the archived record and plan as the answer key.\n\nSet up each run the way the first bench did, in a worktree pair of its own.\n\n## Report\n\nA new report beside the first one, with the same tables: wall time, gate time, fixer time, dollars, ladder and stops, verify, diff and review findings.\nIt checks that the four changes show: no attempt killed by the shuttle timeout, deviations in `summary.md`, and `gate_spans` on the Weaver rows.\nIt explains where Weaver's saving comes from, measured rather than guessed: cache reads per batch for Weaver against cache reads per fork for Webster.\nIt ends with a recommendation on making Weaver tapestry's default implementer.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T11:36:31Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:36:31Z","design_file":"design-weaver-bench-rerun.md","decision_file":"","revision":"dfc21fb993faedf8198d5835ca4c42f84d4fe6fa297ccf87129314965ebbf663"},{"handle":"T21","slug":"autonomy-unattended-level","title":"beam.autonomy takes false, true or unattended, and every reader reads the level","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["dobby","config","cwd","board","orch","slack","loom"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T29"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D1 whole: `LookupAutonomy` in `internal/dobbyengine/tasksetting.go`, `checkTaskSetting` in `internal/dobbyengine/config.go` and its callers, the template comment in `internal/dobbyengine/template.yaml`, `hubgeom.Autonomy` in `internal/hubgeom/autonomy.go` and its callers in slackcli, loomcli, orchcli and beamcli, the beam modes in `internal/boardengine/beammodes.go`, and the autonomy line in `internal/orchengine/prompt.go`, which also serves D9's rule that the line names the level.\nDelivers: The three levels on every path that checks the key, a refusal naming the three values and `lym config beam`, a refused value read as `false` and posted once per episode to the orch, the level on the `autonomy:` stamp, `off`, `on` or `unattended` in the beam modes, and the level named in the orch prompt and the template comment. Every reader that asks only whether autonomy is on reads `true` and `unattended` as on.\nExports: Fixed for consumers: `dobbyengine.AutonomyLevel`, a string type with `AutonomyOff` (\"false\"), `AutonomyOn` (\"true\") and `AutonomyUnattended` (\"unattended\") and an `On() bool` method; `dobbyengine.LookupAutonomy` and `hubgeom.Autonomy` return it.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 260 changed lines)\n- internal/hubgeom (about 70 changed lines)\n- internal/boardengine (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/orchengine (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/orchcli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/slackcli (about 15 changed lines)\n- internal/loomcli (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/beamcli (about 45 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T09:40:38Z","revised":"2026-11-28T09:48:14Z","design_file":"design-autonomy-unattended-level.md","decision_file":"decision-autonomy-unattended-level.md","revision":"4025010b81a72b062b5fc63801fc417402c81cfd53cabd1d78bab25662f7f995"},{"handle":"T22","slug":"window-retile-verb","title":"A lym verb puts the hub windows back in their desktops and tiling zones when they have drifted","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["termwindow","desktop"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T2"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nThe KDE placement task places a lym window only when the window opens, and lym moves no window by itself afterwards.\nA window the operator drags out of its zone, or one KWin moves on its own (seen with two screens, 2026-11-27), stays out of place until it is opened again.\nThe operator asked (2026-11-28) how windows that have come out of sync get tiled again.\n\n## Idea\n\nA verb, run by hand, that re-applies the stored placement to the hub's open lym windows:\neach goes back to its home desktop and zone, tiled, through the same KWin script path and lock the opening placement uses.\nIt moves only lym windows of the hub, and reports what it moved and what it could not place.\n\n## Open\n\n- Name and home of the verb (for example under `lym window`), and whether it covers one worktree or the whole hub.\n- Whether a desktop-bar button runs it.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T08:39:05Z","revised":"2026-11-28T08:45:53Z","design_file":"design-window-retile-verb.md","decision_file":"","revision":"61e7f2288241163108268f3db2847ca5c1699d681f7ef232a5b846971d832cc1"},{"handle":"T24","slug":"fabric-core-teardown","title":"Fabric core: remove, destroy, cleanup, prune, remote teardown and pending teardown move to the core","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","landing"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer A","waits_on":["T23"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the push and origin part's core rules, installer and setters\nDelivers: the rules of remove, cleanup, cleanupremotewarp, prune, archive, ownerarchive, remoteleftover, remoteteardown, pendingteardown and teardownlock in internal/fabriccore; the teardown child as a verb of its own that reads its own snapshot under the teardown lock, takes the handed facts and parent verdicts D2 lists, and pushes every archive tag before any origin delete as a core rule, with PushWithRetryReasons' loop kept inside one action; holdTeardown's in-process path reading the child's snapshot at the child's point; the child's entry point in internal/fabriccli taking the installed seams; destroy.go keeping its guards at execution with no rule moved into it; the pending-teardown record unchanged for fabric status, fabric prune and batten cancel; the D12 move of archiveparent's shed test; a test outside fabric that removes a hubforge pair unrefused with no seam of its own. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests, the heaviest of fabric's, shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: no new API: the teardown verbs keep their public signatures\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 3900 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 3000 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccli (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/pairteardown (about 150 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-teardown.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-teardown.md","revision":"3476fb50ee47d0430bff5de414ffbb651e3760359a5a72f6d2ed99e76711d73b"},{"handle":"T12","slug":"stop-settlement-table","title":"The settlement table of stop classes, loom verbs that wait for the driver to park, and the driver's halt count","kind":"task","types":["feature","enhancement"],"areas":["shed","loom","batten"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer B","waits_on":["T21"],"brief":"","body":"## The settlement table: stop classes and who settles each, per autonomy level and halt count\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D2's table and its verdict order; nothing from another part.\nDelivers: D2's table steps: the new leaf package `internal/stopclass`, standard library only and no I/O, holding one named class constant per row of D2's table and the one function that returns a verdict for a class, a level and the halt count at the row, read in D2's order. lym's bound per class lives here, and lym's column applies at every level. The driver rows that take their bound from it, and the `settle: orch` hand-back, are built by `driver-settles-stops`.\nExports: Fixed for consumers: `stopclass.Class` with one constant per row; `stopclass.Level` with `LevelOff` (\"false\"), `LevelOn` (\"true\") and `LevelUnattended` (\"unattended\"), the same strings `dobbyengine.AutonomyLevel` holds, so a consumer converts with `stopclass.Level(level)`; `stopclass.Verdict` with `VerdictLym`, `VerdictOrch`, `VerdictOperator`; `stopclass.Settle(class Class, level Level, haltsAtRow, lymSettles int) Decision`, `Decision{Verdict, Bound}`.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/stopclass (about 480 changed lines)\n\n## lym loom resume and lym loom start wait for the driver to park, with a no-wait option batten passes\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D5 whole: the `shedrun.StartNotParkedKind` refusals in `internal/loomcli/resume.go` and `internal/loomcli/start.go`, the driver's wait record `sheddriver.ReadDriveWait` that push-retry-not-halt added, the keep-warm cap from `shuttleengines.KeepWarm`, `childSpawnError` in `internal/battencli/wire.go` and the `battenshed.ErrChildNotParked` retry in `internal/battenshed/innerrun.go`.\nDelivers: Both verbs wait for the park, or for a run lock on a non-running run to be released, under the keep-warm cap, waking at most once a second with an injected clock; answer at once for a driver that keeps a wait record; refuse past the cap naming the retry; and refuse at once with the no-wait option, which batten passes on its child start and on every resume it runs, the resume of dobby-pause-lift-resume's lift pass among them.\nExports: Fixed for consumers: the `--no-wait` flag on `lym loom resume` and `lym loom start`.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/loomcli (about 420 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 60 changed lines)\n- internal/battenshed (about 50 changed lines)\n\n## The driver gives each stop an identity, counts halts at a row with lym's retries, and records a halt's four facts\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D3 whole and D7's recording steps; `stopclass.Settle` and the class constants from stop-settlement-table; `dobbyengine.AutonomyLevel` through `hubgeom.Autonomy` from autonomy-unattended-level, converted with `stopclass.Level(level)` in the driver wiring in `internal/loomcli/drivewiring.go`. It answers the Discussion-Review round-3 BLOCKING finding failed-state-halt-not-counted-or-recorded through the stop identity, as both added decisions rule.\nDelivers: D3: the stop record written through `internal/state` beside the settle records, holding the open stop's key, its halt entry's history index and the four facts; the identity opened, closed and taken over across a driver restart as D3 says; settle records carrying the stop identity, the class and the halt entry's index; one function that computes both the halt count at a row and lym's settles of a class at the row over the whole history, counting and excluding exactly what D3 lists, so `wakeRestartGap` never restores a bound. D7's recording steps: every hand-back records `class:`, `settle:`, `halts_at_row:` and `halt:` on its history entry, or on the stop record when the stop left none, and carries them on the notice beside `autonomy:`; every hand-back to the orch reads `settle: orch` or `settle: operator`. D7's criterion, which needs the dobby's child read, is carried by severe-halt-slack-post.\nExports: Fixed for consumers: `shedengine.HistoryEntry` fields `Class`, `Settle`, `HaltsAtRow` and `Halt`; `shedrun.SettleRecord` fields `StopID`, `Class` and `HistoryIndex`; the stop record type and its path helper in `internal/shedrun`; the halt key, the row's producer and the stop identity joined, which readers treat as an opaque string; the count function in `internal/sheddriver`.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/sheddriver (about 820 changed lines)\n- internal/shedrun (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 90 changed lines)\n- internal/loomcli (about 40 changed lines)\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T09:40:38Z","revised":"2026-11-28T09:47:40Z","design_file":"design-stop-settlement-table.md","decision_file":"decision-stop-settlement-table.md","revision":"59b1b19b8a707a2f6425a5929216c98041192d34f9d04e285f23f29d702ab77a"},{"handle":"T25","slug":"fabric-core-merge-landing","title":"Fabric core: merge-in, mid-merge, handover, takeover, integrate, task patch and discard move to the core","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","batten","landing"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer B","waits_on":["T24"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the teardown part's tree, with the installer and the shed adapter\nDelivers: the rules of merge, mergeguards, mergelifecycle, mergestate, mergestateactive, mergestage, mergepaths, mergeerrors, midmerge, handover, takeover, takeoverwrite, integrateweft, taskpatch and taskbranchdiscard in internal/fabriccore; the shedengine.Status and shedengine.Takeover uses cut: OriginParentRunStatus and OriginBattenStatus return bytes and found, their callers decode through shed, ReadUpstreamTakeover and the result types that carried shedengine.Takeover carry fabric's own takeover entry of machine, subject and time filled by the seam's decoder, and the takeover write stays a state.WriteJSON patch of the one takeover key; the shed-module test written against today's code before the cut that pins the takeover write's status bytes, an undeclared key included, and the JSON-key parity of fabric's and shed's takeover types; the D12 moves of handover, takeoverrun and rejectedtakeover's shed tests to the shed module. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: fabric's takeover entry type in internal/fabriclayout and the takeover decoder seam\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 3000 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 900 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/landingshed (about 100 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-merge-landing.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-merge-landing.md","revision":"5a86589de788f09eb1965656a90b1ed5b84f781f1c921483e598afd6966b6799"},{"handle":"T26","slug":"fabric-core-pull-replay","title":"Fabric core: pull, replay, bolt, reconcile, parent tips and warp forward move to the core; stencil reads leave fabric","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","shed","stencil","board","heddle"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer C","waits_on":["T25"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the merge and landing part's tree, with the takeover entry type and the shed adapter\nDelivers: the rules of pull, hubpull, replay, bolt, boltreplay, reconcile, parenttips, codetipsettle, upstreamrun, stencilcommit and warpforward's fabric side in internal/fabriccore; the code-tip settle handing the seam the previous transition's status bytes with the status and lock paths, shed's adapter writing them through state.WriteJSON, and the shed-module test before the cut that pins its status bytes; upstreamrun's done verdict asked of the seam's decoder; replay's and pull's shedrun uses through the run-path seam; StencilSource and StencilBaseByStamp moved to internal/stencilsource (new) over fabricengine.HeadContains and the path-taking reads fabric adds (commit exists, ancestor of, a path's revisions, a file at a revision), with internal/stencilcli and cmd/lym's stencilseed retargeted and their two integration tests moved with them; the D12 moves of the bolt, pull and replay tests that drive boardengine, boardkit or heddleengine. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: internal/stencilsource's StencilSource and StencilBaseByStamp; fabricengine's commit-exists, ancestor, path-revisions and file-at-revision reads\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 3200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 100 changed lines)\n- internal/stencilsource (about 700 changed lines)\n- internal/stencilcli (about 40 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/shedengine (about 400 changed lines)\n- internal/boardengine (about 900 changed lines)\n- internal/heddleengine (about 200 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-pull-replay.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-pull-replay.md","revision":"e15e15891f55dadb9e32b142443aba85f6e39417dc14235d7fc31fae768fe04b"},{"handle":"T33","slug":"driver-settles-stops","title":"The driver and the arbiter settle known stops and budget escalations themselves within the table's bound","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["shed","board","batten","shuttle","webster","arbiter","loom","stencil"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer C","waits_on":["T12"],"brief":"","body":"## The driver settles board claim refusals, failed launches, bounce budgets and gate attempts itself within the table's bound\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D4's driver rows, D2's driver steps and D3's bound check, over stop-halt-count's count function and stop record and stop-settlement-table's bounds. Raise sites: the claim push in `pushOwnerCommit` in `internal/boardengine/ownerset.go`, reached from `claimBoardEntry` in `internal/battencli/owner.go`; `shuttleengine.ErrNotStarted` and `shedtransient.TransientAgentStart`; the session timeout reason in `internal/shedadapters/multillm.go`; the fixer fork in `internal/websterengine`; `strandGoneError` in `internal/shuttleengine/run.go`; and the gate-attempts Stuck from `gateFailedReason` in `internal/shedadapters/gateattempts.go`.\nDelivers: New rows in `decideDrive` in `internal/sheddriver/driverules.go`: a board claim refusal gets a board sync and a re-step; a launch never ready, a session timeout, a failed fixer fork and a lost strand get one fresh start of the row's producer; a bounce budget exhausted gets one resume, matching the `shedengine.ReasonBounceBudgetExhausted` prefix; a gate-attempts Stuck gets a re-step with a fresh producer session. Each class but the bounce budget's is raised as a named kind or field where the stop starts. Each row takes its bound from the table, a settle within the bound sends no notice and leaves a settle record, and a stop past it is handed back with its verdict. The tackler keeps every stop no row names.\nExports: The named kinds for the claim refusal, the session timeout, the fixer fork failure, the lost strand and the gate-attempts Stuck.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/sheddriver (about 650 changed lines)\n- internal/boardengine (about 70 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine (about 130 changed lines)\n- internal/shedadapters (about 140 changed lines)\n- internal/shedtransient (about 50 changed lines)\n- internal/websterengine (about 80 changed lines)\n\n## The arbiter takes budget escalations, and nothing lands a round with an open blocking finding unattended\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D6's Go and arbiter-stencil steps: `Eligibility` and `Stop` in `internal/arbiterstop/arbiterstop.go`, built in `arbiterstop.Check` from `NewReaders`; the arbiter call in `internal/shedarbiter`; the stencil `contracts/stencils/loom/loom-template-arbiter.md`; the Bouncer's escalation and accept paths in `internal/shedadapters/bouncer.go` and `readCirclingFile` and `recordAutoContinue` in `internal/shedadapters/circling.go`; `circlingVerb` and `finalizeCirclingVerb` in `internal/loomcli/circling.go`; the fixer report's `deferred` disposition parsed by `internal/burlerengine/fixerreport.go`; `dobbyengine.LookupAutonomy` from autonomy-unattended-level.\nDelivers: Cause `budget` admitted; `arbiterstop.Stop` carrying the run's level, named in the arbiter's prompt; the arbiter's `budget` rules in D6's order, with the one continue per segment read from the circling decision files and refused at the hard cap; one board note for a deferred-findings accept at Code-Review and after, filed by the driver through a seam the loomcli driver wiring backs with `internal/boardengine`, with no board grant for the arbiter; under `unattended` the Bouncer runs every perch as with `blocking_escalates`, and neither the arbiter at any cause nor a Finalize fix-review accept lands a round with an open BLOCKING finding or a red gate. The orch's side of D6, the hold and the role text, is built by orch-unattended-rules.\nExports: The `Autonomy` field on `arbiterstop.Stop`.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/arbiterstop (about 160 changed lines)\n- internal/shedarbiter (about 90 changed lines)\n- internal/shedadapters (about 230 changed lines)\n- internal/sheddriver (about 160 changed lines)\n- internal/loomcli (about 150 changed lines)\n- contracts/stencils/loom (about 60 changed lines)\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T09:40:38Z","revised":"2026-11-28T09:47:41Z","design_file":"design-driver-settles-stops.md","decision_file":"decision-driver-settles-stops.md","revision":"10cf4aa70713173ee62fd7bbd88b16f8e9c2d35d38ded559a0bc47698502f876"},{"handle":"T27","slug":"fabric-core-add-clone","title":"Fabric core: add, clone, adopt, junctions, weft wiring and the hub workspace move to the core; the vscode import is cut","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","ide","batten","heddle","reconcile","shed"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer D","waits_on":["T26"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the pull and replay part's tree, with every earlier seam and the installer\nDelivers: the rules of add, addadopt, addadoptonly, addanchor, addparent, clone, checkout, checkoutstate, forkrecords, gitexclude, warpexclude, junction, junctionnames, pairbranch, pairparts, resetsubpathcheck, scratchlink, shortnamebinding, shortnamecommit, unwire, weftwiring and hubworkspace in internal/fabriccore; Add's rollback as the core's inverse action list over executed actions and pre-creation facts, the engine keeping createdToken and createdBranchToken for destroy.go, with a tier1 rollback on a real hub; add's, addanchor's and forkrecords' shedrun uses through the run-path seam; hubworkspace's vscode import cut by the workspace builder seam, installed by cmd/lym/main.go beside InstallPairFolderNamer and replacing InstallWorkspaceContent, called on every exit that regenerates the workspace today, a rollback included, with nil writing no file; the IDE module's builder and its test in internal/ideengine; builder installs in the tests outside fabric that read the hub workspace file, internal/ideengine/spawn_hub_integration_test.go and cmd/lym/prerunseams_test.go among them; internal/beamcli/dobbyleftover_integration_test.go dropping t.Parallel; the cmd/lym test that runs the binary on a hub with every seam installed; the D12 moves of the clone, worktreeseam and add_runrecords tests that drive fabriccli, heddleengine, hubreconcile, boardkit or shedrun. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests, the largest of fabric's, shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: fabricengine's workspace builder seam and its setter\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 4100 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 2800 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccli (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/ideengine (about 400 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/beamcli (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/heddleengine (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/hubreconcile (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/shedrun (about 170 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-add-clone.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-add-clone.md","revision":"357d93b095fbb86877a439f775411546fbf46ff21daf6cad82c754b632e89a75"},{"handle":"T32","slug":"dobby-pause-lift-resume","title":"The dobby resumes lifted pauses, posts severe halts to Slack, keeps quiet on correct waits and chases unsettled stalls","kind":"task","types":["feature","enhancement"],"areas":["dobby","tender","batten","slack","creel","shuttle","loom","config"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer D","waits_on":["T33","T2"],"brief":"","body":"## The dobby resumes a run handed back for a pause once the pause lifts, and never a run the orch holds\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D4's pause-lift and hold steps: `liftPending` and `spawn` in `internal/dobbyengine/passes.go`, `syncBoltHold` in `internal/dobbyengine/catchupwait.go`, `Pause` in `internal/tenderengine/pause.go` and `pauserecord.Entry`; the `--no-wait` flag from loom-verbs-await-park, which batten passes on the lift pass's child resume.\nDelivers: The lift resumes a run whose last stop is the hand-back for the lifted pause, matched by scope, with a dobby pass on a run that is not drivable, keeping the lift in the dobby state record until the resume succeeds or the run moves on; a hold reason on a pause entry, set on a live entry of the scope when one exists, that stands through a `lym tender resume` with no slug and is lifted only by one naming the slug; a bolt-hold entry carrying a hold reason replaced by the dobby's own hold entry when it lifts the bolt-hold. The driver rows of D4 are built by driver-settles-stops.\nExports: Fixed for consumers: the hold reason field on `pauserecord.Entry` and the tender pause option that sets it, which the orch's hold of orch-unattended-rules uses.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 480 changed lines)\n- internal/tenderengine (about 220 changed lines)\n- internal/pauserecord (about 50 changed lines)\n\n## The dobby posts each severe halt to Slack, and the relay mentions the operator on a severe-halt post\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D7's dobby steps and D8's relay steps: the four facts and the halt key stop-halt-count records on the history entry or the stop record; `ChildReading` in `internal/dobbyengine/runnotice.go`, read through `ReadChild` in `internal/battencli/dobbynotice.go`; the program post `internal/dobbyengine/waitreminder.go` makes, wired in `internal/beamcli/dobbydeps.go`; `internal/slackengine/outbound.go` and the operator ID in `slackengine.Setup`; `dobbyengine.AutonomyLevel` from autonomy-unattended-level.\nDelivers: D7: `ChildReading` carries the four facts from the halt entry the stop record names, or from the stop record; the dobby that drives the run posts one `[operator] severe halt \u003cslug\u003e \u003ckey\u003e:` line to `\u003cshortname\u003e:SLACK` under its program name for each `settle: operator` halt under `true` or `unattended`, whether or not an orch strand is recorded, and keeps the posted key in its state record, written after the post. D8: the relay mentions the configured operator on a post whose first line opens `[operator] severe halt \u003cslug\u003e`, from an orch or a program, and dedups a second one by slug and key within `slack.remind_after_min`, keyless only against keyless; every other post carries no mention. D8's reminders and D10's stall posts are built by dobby-stall-chase; the orch's relay rules by orch-unattended-rules.\nExports: The severe-post record in the dobby state record, which dobby-stall-chase's reminders and stall posts read.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 420 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 70 changed lines)\n- internal/beamcli (about 60 changed lines)\n- internal/slackengine (about 260 changed lines)\n\n## The idle and held notices skip correct waits and name what an agent waits on\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D11 whole: the quiet notice in `internal/dobbyengine/runnotice.go`, `readAgentActivity` in `internal/battencli/wire.go`, `Store.AwaitsReply` in `internal/creelengine/verbs.go` and `creelcli.AwaitsReplyProbe`, the held notice in `internal/shuttleengine/hold.go` and `Runner.SetReplyProbe` in `internal/shuttleengine/run.go`, and `verifyBudgetNotice` in `internal/loomshed/verifybudget.go`.\nDelivers: The quiet notice raises nothing for a `Raddle-Write` run whose proposal passes with a live raddle agent or for an agent with an open question in creel, and names an unread reply; `AwaitsReply` read as two probes for the quiet notice while the held notice keeps the combined one; an idle notice names the live command, its run time and its last output, or the Finalize step and what it waits on; the held notice is not sent while a question is open or a reply unread, in every runner; the verify gate's past-budget notice names the failing runs or says none failed.\nExports: Fixed for consumers: `creelcli.OpenQuestionProbe` and `creelcli.UnreadReplyProbe`, which dobby-stall-chase's question chase reads.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 130 changed lines)\n- internal/creelengine (about 130 changed lines)\n- internal/creelcli (about 50 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine (about 160 changed lines)\n- internal/loomshed (about 40 changed lines)\n\n## The dobby chases stalls the orch leaves unsettled, reminds of severe posts, and warns when autonomy cannot reach the operator\n\nFiled from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D10 and D12 whole and D8's reminder steps: severe-halt-slack-post's severe-post record and post; `creelcli.OpenQuestionProbe` from idle-notice-correct-waits; the respawn cap the child read shows; the dobby `ConfigSpec` in `internal/dobbyengine/config.go`; the slack button state and the configured operator, read through the dobby's wiring in `internal/beamcli/dobbydeps.go`.\nDelivers: D10: `beam.halt_chase_min`, default 10, classified `CategoryNone` and refused under 1 naming the key in full; one chase line per stall key to the dobby's orch for a `settle: orch` halt, an open creel question and a dead driver past its caps, naming `lym loom resume` when a parent decision came after the halt; under `unattended` one severe post per stall key one interval later, also sent to the orch while its slack button is off; chase and post kept in the state record and ended by D10's end events. D8: the wait reminders remind of each severe post the state record holds at `slack.remind_after_min`, without a mention, until its end event. D12: one warning per episode when a task resolves `true` or `unattended` with the slack button off or no operator configured, naming `lym slack`.\nExports: The `beam.halt_chase_min` key.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 620 changed lines)\n- internal/beamcli (about 90 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 40 changed lines)\n\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T09:40:38Z","revised":"2026-11-28T09:47:43Z","design_file":"design-dobby-pause-lift-resume.md","decision_file":"decision-dobby-pause-lift-resume.md","revision":"1b5329546d632aef2341b4a07ead4bdd061ed1d8af6f468829446827fd6e658b"},{"handle":"T28","slug":"fabric-core-records-guards","title":"Fabric core: anchors, records store, commit, trailers and index move to the core; import guards, PATTERN close-out and remeasure","kind":"task","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["fabric","testkit","pattern","docs"],"status":"","project":"P4","section":"Layer E","waits_on":["T27"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `fabric-git-free-core`.\n\nTakes: the add and clone part's tree, every other concern moved\nDelivers: the rules of anchor, anchorchange, anchormarker, anchormove, anchorstop, anchortarget, commit, committedfile, commitweftpaths, index, moverecord, recordsgit, recordslayout, recordsprobe, recordsreads, snapshotuncommitted, trailer and trailercommit in internal/fabriccore, Commit's secret-path refusal as a pre-lock snapshot fact the engine fills from internal/secretpath's matcher; the cross-verb livestate, onerepository_lifecycle and droplocal tests and export_test.go reduced to what no other test guards; the tier0 guard on test imports of the three fabric packages (D12) and the tier0 guard on their production dependency closure (D13); PATTERN-fabric-boundary's rule line and the new PATTERN-fabric-core entry beside the guards they cite; the remeasure doc under docs/research/speed/, measured in one session against a checkout of the commit before the foundation part that the implementer asks its parent for, which also records fabric's test lines before and after. Under the parent's added entry 'The restructure cuts fabric's test code, it does not only move it', this concern's tests and the cross-verb tests shrink: a test is kept or moved only where it alone guards a rule, at the lowest tier that can check it, duplicates and the same rule tested at several tiers are deleted, not moved, and the change description lists every deleted test and, for each kept or moved test, the rule it alone guards\nExports: the two import guards\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/fabricengine (about 2200 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriccore (about 1800 changed lines)\n- internal/fabriclayout (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/fabrickit (about 100 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 100 changed lines)\n- docs/research/speed (about 250 changed lines)\n\n## Added by the parent (2026-11-27)\n\nThe foundation part leaves temporary `testOnlyCodeAllowed` entries for chain functions whose first production caller is a later part (its record entry \"A chain part may leave production code that only a named later part first calls\").\nThis part's PATTERN close-out also states that allowance in `pattern/PATTERN-test-only-code.md` under `## Rule` for as long as such entries exist, or, once every entry is gone, checks that no entry of that kind is left and that the PATTERN text needs none.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T21:44:40Z","revised":"2026-11-27T12:22:58Z","design_file":"design-fabric-core-records-guards.md","decision_file":"decision-fabric-core-records-guards.md","revision":"3e9bf5af6c28147545793f9413aa24b52e82febcfddd42e5e9dab36a22aadcc4"},{"handle":"T38","slug":"orch-unattended-rules","title":"The orch role texts gain the unattended level's rules and the glossary names the level","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["orch","heddle"],"status":"","project":"P3","section":"Layer E","waits_on":["T33","T32"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `self-healing-runs`.\n\nTakes: D9 and D13 whole and the role-text steps of D6 and D7, written in `contracts/orchrole/role-autonomy.md`, `contracts/orchrole/orch-role.md` and `internal/heddleengine/glossary.md`; the hold reason from dobby-pause-lift-resume, the arbiter's hand-on from arbiter-budget-escalations, the `settle: operator` post from severe-halt-slack-post and the stall posts from dobby-stall-chase, each landed. D9's autonomy line in `internal/orchengine/prompt.go` is built by autonomy-unattended-level.\nDelivers: The unattended rules D9 lists; D6's hold of a round with an open BLOCKING finding or a red gate and of a Finalize halt while one remains; D7's relay rule, keyed posts, the in-pane relay while the slack button is off, the hold after a severe relay and the `lym selfreport create` filing, with \"lym classifies no halt\" dropped; and D13's three glossary lines.\nExports: Nothing in Go.\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- contracts/orchrole (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/heddleengine (about 10 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-28T09:40:38Z","revised":"2026-11-28T09:47:43Z","design_file":"design-orch-unattended-rules.md","decision_file":"decision-orch-unattended-rules.md","revision":"800e12e13aeea877c05b3c1a9e8b089995936bc6a95de9d343ab4b339fa3607b"},{"handle":"T17","slug":"keep-landed-run-records","title":"Keep each landed run's findings, asks and fixer reports in its archive tag, with an index row and lym report landed","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["landing","fabric","report","loom","burler","shed","cwd","git","testkit","pattern"],"status":"","project":"P6","section":"Layer F","waits_on":["T28"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `keep-landed-records`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record whole: the archive tags teardown pushes, the loom status commit and run-records set, the parent-review ask record's locked updates in internal/burlerengine, the landing fixer and fix review scratch reports, and Finalize's merge-in, merge and push steps\nDelivers: one kept directory under each run's _lym/ that the status commit stages and the archive tag carries, holding PR findings copies, ask record and report copies seeded back after an adopt, landing fixer and fix review report copies, the landing record and the index row; and a read-only lym report landed verb that lists and extracts every archived run's kept records\nExports: the kept directory's name in internal/lymdirs; the landing record and index row types with their writers and one reader in internal/shedrun; the first-parent landing searches in internal/fabricengine; pullrequest.API.Merge returning the merge commit SHA; the cobra verb lym report landed\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/lymdirs (about 10 changed lines)\n- internal/loomengine (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/loomcli (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/fabricengine (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/burlerengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/landingshed (about 450 changed lines)\n- internal/pullrequest (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/pairteardown (about 5 changed lines)\n- internal/testkit/prkit (about 5 changed lines)\n- internal/shedrun (about 140 changed lines)\n- internal/gitrepo (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/reportengine (about 430 changed lines)\n- internal/reportcli (about 170 changed lines)\n- PATTERN.md (about 4 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:21Z","revised":"2026-11-27T09:46:36Z","design_file":"design-keep-landed-run-records.md","decision_file":"decision-keep-landed-run-records.md","revision":"2471b6479211efb760aac8755a77bc491c027985ca96c2fbe17eccd47aff0523"},{"handle":"T7","slug":"area-creel-identity","title":"creel across an area: kernel-attached sender identity, the area registry and the hub directory","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["creel","cwd"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the Sketch's decision record, D8 whole and the pieces of D3 and D6 that D8 rests on; peerPID (internal/creelengine/peer_linux.go), daemon.resolve and resolveSender, serveHubCall (receive.go), the outbound worker's spawn closure (outbound.go attempt), hubregistry's Entry, Lookup, Live and RepositoryName, creelcli's hubdirectory and its one machine-record write\nDelivers: every creel daemon sets SO_PASSCRED and SO_PASSPIDFD and takes the sender from SCM_CREDENTIALS and SCM_PIDFD with the pidfd liveness check; D3's inside test (barrier in /proc/\u003cpid\u003e/status or attr/current, plus a cgroup naming a scope of the hub's area by the whole-name forms lym-area-\u003chub key\u003e.scope and lym-area-\u003chub key\u003e-\u003cpane|run|attach\u003e-\u003cpid\u003e.scope), with which a daemon serves only deliver and confirm to a peer inside another hub's area or a peer gone or unreadable; the machine record moved to the hub directory \u003cper-user lym state dir\u003e/hubs/ with its lock, read under a shared lock on a read-only descriptor, with the old hubs.json read for entries the new record lacks; each daemon writing its shortname, creel socket path, repository name and the area-aware creel level into its entry, bar an in-area daemon; the area registry area-hubs.json and its lock in the hub directory, its reader, and a writer the core part's lym area start calls; hubregistry.Lookup, the confirm's geometry and RepositoryName reading an area hub's registry entry and, inside an area, every other hub's host-written record, never a Bolt shortname file or a prime's .git/config; every registry reader and writer dropping an entry whose area file ~/.config/lym/area-\u003chub key\u003e.yaml is gone (Added after Discussion); the outbound worker starting no other hub's daemon inside an area, and a hub without an area starting no area hub's daemon; PATTERN-agent-addressing's Across hubs background gaining the area-registry repository rule\nExports: areaengine.HubKey (reedengine.ServerName of the canonical hub path), areaengine.FilePath(hubKey), areaengine.ScopeName and the scope-name parser, areaengine.Inside(pid, hubKey, barrier); hubregistry.AreaEntry {hub_path, shortname, creel_socket, repository}, hubregistry.ReadAreaRegistry and hubregistry.WriteAreaEntry, the hub-directory path helper; the area-aware creel level constant D5 admits\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/creelengine (about 450 changed lines)\n- internal/hubregistry (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/creelcli (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/areaengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/lymdirs (about 20 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 30 changed lines)\n- internal/slackcli (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-creel-identity.md","decision_file":"decision-area-creel-identity.md","revision":"4d56faff3481a8de1737a1276c5f4a0babbea1a7acc787ab152a3389b5c0712b"},{"handle":"T14","slug":"area-seccomp-core","title":"Stage 1 of the closed area: supervisor, Landlock ruleset, connect filter, attach client and the in-area engines, token and relay","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["reed","shuttle","claude","codex","creel","dobby","slack","process","cwd","git","cli","docs","pattern"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T7"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the creel identity part's exports (inside test, scope names, area registry writer, hub directory, area-aware level); config-keys-nested's rewritten PATTERN-config-layers line; docs/research/platform/seccomp-connect-probe/main.go as D4's starting point; reedengine's server spawn and scrub (lifecycle.go), reedcli's attach (EnsureSession, Status, the watchdog spawn), proc.CapProcessMemory and RunMemoryCapped, lymtemp's root and run directories, runproc's end verbs, claudeengine's account resolution, TranscriptReader and user-setup source, codexengine's homes and links, githubclient's token chain, slackengine's EnsureRelay, StopRelay and file geometry, creelcli's SendReady\nDelivers: lym area init, start, stop, show, supervise, exec, git-credential and slack-setup; the area file outside the hub and its write-grant refusals; the supervisor in its own scope with its record, socket, SO_PEERCRED check, subreaping and verbs boot, attach-scope, cap-memory, kill-scope and slack-relay (start, stop, presence, status); the one launcher setting no_new_privs, Landlock ABI 8 with TSYNC and the filter with TSYNC, TSYNC_ESRCH and NEW_LISTENER; the connect decision on the checked inode and the derived allow list; the per-hub area root under lymtemp; the host-side attach client under the area; lym area start writing the machine record and the registry entry for an area hub; the host-side refusal of D7 as the first root pre-run step and at every hub selection, with machine walks skipping area hubs; dobby and creel spawn sites refusing from outside; the memory caps through the supervisor; the area's own engine config directories, GitHub token and relay records; README.md, docs/overview.md and the glossary per D16, the pseudo-terminal bound included (Added after Discussion); PATTERN-closed-area added, PATTERN-child-processes changed, and the backgrounds of PATTERN-child-processes, PATTERN-lym-files and PATTERN-config-layers per the notes for the plan writer; the seccomp half of the under-each-barrier criteria, whose apparmor half the fallback part carries; and the D6 attach criterion's second clause, which the record's unindented continuation line cuts from the parsed bullet: a `detach-client -E` sent to the attach client runs its command under the area's filter and in the attach scope\nExports: the supervisor's verb protocol and its client in internal/areaengine (one request per connection, the verbs named above), the launcher's start-request protocol, the area file type and its loader, the barrier enum seccomp and apparmor, and the start check's hook for a barrier-specific refusal, on which the fallback and host-exit parts add verbs and a barrier\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 3000 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 500 changed lines)\n- cmd/lym (about 150 changed lines)\n- internal/reedengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 400 changed lines)\n- internal/proc (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/lymtemp (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/runproc (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/dobbyengine (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/creelcli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/machinecli (about 80 changed lines)\n- internal/hubregistry (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine/claudeengine (about 250 changed lines)\n- internal/shuttleengine/codexengine (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/githubclient (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/slackengine (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/slackcli (about 120 changed lines)\n- internal/hubgeom (about 80 changed lines)\n- README.md (about 80 changed lines)\n- docs (about 80 changed lines)\n- pattern (about 40 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"tapestry","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T12:03:20Z","design_file":"design-area-seccomp-core.md","decision_file":"decision-area-seccomp-core.md","revision":"f8198d031fb9211c0251d68bbd1f5fc701b4a37b1c951e0eacb697699dd484be"},{"handle":"T15","slug":"area-apparmor-barrier","title":"The closed area's AppArmor fallback barrier, built only when the OSL-1046 probe fails","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["process"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T14"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the core part's supervisor, launcher, attach client, area file with its barrier key, and the start check's barrier hook; the OSL-1046 probe result of D14 step 1\nDelivers: lym area profile rendering the hub's profile from the area file's lists and the Landlock paths, with other hubs' creel sockets as one creel-*.sock pattern and the name a hash of the text, and naming the root load command; the launcher and attach client entering it through aa-exec, each still installing a filter with D4's foreign-ABI, io_uring_setup and clone3 rules, no connect rule, no listener, and TSYNC with TSYNC_ESRCH (Added after Discussion); the start refusing while no loaded profile carries the rendered name; the inside test's apparmor label branch exercised end to end\nExports: areaengine's apparmor barrier and its profile renderer\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 350 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 100 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-apparmor-barrier.md","decision_file":"decision-area-apparmor-barrier.md","revision":"2cd8c22abd358be34dc337618b3e0a9099c807378e35e79a040f22ae5bd411ac"},{"handle":"T16","slug":"area-host-exits","title":"Stage 2 of the closed area: window, hibernate and deploy as supervisor verbs, and lym ide refused inside","kind":"task","types":["feature"],"areas":["termwindow","hibernate","batten","ide","deploy","reed"],"status":"draft","project":"P7","section":"Draft","waits_on":["T15"],"brief":"","body":"Filed from the Sketch `sandbox-closed-area-build`.\n\nTakes: the core part's supervisor verb protocol and attach client; termwindow's Launcher.OpenAttach, hibernatecli's request and its systemctl spawn, battenshed's LandDeps.RunDeploy and its wiring, idecli; the operator's stop of D14 step 3\nDelivers: the supervisor's window verb (direct child of the hub root, title built from the directory name, Konsole running the host's lym reed attach with no shell after it) with lym reed window routed to it inside an area; the hibernate verb running systemctl hibernate alone; the deploy verb recording a pending deploy from Batten's Land row with a notice; lym area deploy cloning https://github.com/\u003crepository\u003e.git into the area state directory, fetching the area file's deploy_branch, checking the commit is on it and running the area file's deploy command with the host's toolchain and caches, refusing with the way forward when repository, deploy or deploy_branch is missing, and lym area init writing deploy_branch as main (Added after Discussion, with its acceptance criterion that a rewritten origin and a hub record naming another branch change neither); lym ide refusing inside an area\nExports: the window, hibernate and deploy verbs and their clients\n\nThe part changes these packages:\n\n- internal/areaengine (about 300 changed lines)\n- internal/areacli (about 200 changed lines)\n- internal/termwindow (about 50 changed lines)\n- internal/reedcli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/hibernatecli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/battenshed (about 60 changed lines)\n- internal/battencli (about 40 changed lines)\n- internal/idecli (about 20 changed lines)\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"darn","created":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","revised":"2026-11-26T11:58:18Z","design_file":"design-area-host-exits.md","decision_file":"decision-area-host-exits.md","revision":"a3b15d5602b78c496bf04602933ffdee0cea5c686924e38df257f3c0e44152c4"},{"handle":"E1","slug":"pr-review-github-recorded","title":"PR-Review session tests drive pullrequest.Client over a recorded GitHub stand-in instead of prkit.API","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["testkit","landing"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nThe Code-Review of `pr-session-mail-stand-ins` (round 1, finding F2) found that its new tier1 PR-Review mail test adds uses of `prkit.API`,\nwhose stand-in inventory verdict is \"goes\".\nThe tree has no recorded GitHub stand-in to drive `pullrequest.Client` over, so the parent accepted the use for that task.\n\n## Idea\n\nGive testkit a recorded GitHub stand-in, in the manner of the recorded tmux kit, that `pullrequest.Client` runs over,\nand move the PR-Review session tests off `prkit.API` onto it.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T10:32:32Z","revised":"2026-11-28T10:32:32Z","design_file":"design-pr-review-github-recorded.md","decision_file":"","revision":"1f0b220c674a74c2e6fe966db2a4366287fdfc5431bed0cbf665ca525fe5957f"},{"handle":"E2","slug":"creel-delivery-reed-tier1","title":"A tier1 creel delivery backed by real reed, so tests stop listing strands by hand in creelfake.Delivery","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["testkit","creel","reed"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nThe Code-Review of `pr-session-mail-stand-ins` (round 1, finding F2) found that its new tier1 test reaches `creelfake.Delivery` through `startNoticeOrch`,\nwhose inventory verdict is \"real\", and that the halt row's unknown-strand refusal rests on the fake delivery's hand-written strand list.\nThe tree has no reed-backed creel delivery at tier1, so the parent accepted the use for that task.\n\n## Idea\n\nGive tier1 tests a creel delivery backed by a real reed, or by the recorded tmux kit, so a strand is known because reed holds it,\nand move `startNoticeOrch` and its callers onto it.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T10:32:33Z","revised":"2026-11-28T10:32:33Z","design_file":"design-creel-delivery-reed-tier1.md","decision_file":"","revision":"89dd0bb265f30becba4a9372f38eaa553e94c140596cbb108de93ebb255686c6"},{"handle":"E3","slug":"pr-mail-test-wired-seams","title":"The PR-Review mail test takes every git, pause and record seam from loomcli wiring instead of envkit stand-ins","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["testkit","landing"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":["T5"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nCode-Review round 2 of `pr-session-mail-stand-ins` (finding F10, BLOCKING) was closed by an accepted decision with the finding unfixed.\n`prReviewMailDeps` takes only `Mode`, `Origin` and `TaskHead` from `wired.PullRequest`.\nIt still reads envkit's hand-written `DirtyPaths` (nil), `CommitsMissing` (nil), `TaskHasCommits` (true), `PauseCovered` (false) and `RecordChanges` (nil channel),\nstanding in for verifytree, fabricengine, the pause record and the record watch over a real pair.\n\n## Idea\n\nAssign those seams from `wired.PullRequest` as well, and make the state a wired seam needs through gitkit or fabric in the test setup,\nsuch as a commit on the pair's HEAD beyond the parent branch for `TaskHasCommits`.\nKeep `RecordPath` and `RecordLockPath` in step with the wired record paths.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T11:41:45Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:41:50Z","design_file":"design-pr-mail-test-wired-seams.md","decision_file":"","revision":"3c8243162b0996bc073f951b59bec020324abe182f2bacc6f49af804582d35fc"},{"handle":"E4","slug":"card-gate-no-guard-step","title":"The card gate leaves the guard step to the batch gate","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["gate","webster","weaver"],"status":"","project":"P8","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":["T9"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (`docs/research/webster/webster-time-2026-11-08.md`, lever 1)\n\nThe guard step was 7,043 of the card gate's 9,117 s (77%), run 787 times for 226 cards, while the batch gate already runs it.\nDropping it from the card gate saved an estimated 6 to 8 min per Webster row; its failures would surface at the batch gate, about 60 s a fix round.\n\n## Idea\n\nRun the guard step only in the batch gate, for Webster and Weaver alike.\nKeep or drop this note on the figures the implementer gate-time measurement gives.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T12:06:16Z","revised":"2026-11-28T12:06:17Z","design_file":"design-card-gate-no-guard-step.md","decision_file":"","revision":"9508604df05387a718c2947731923f3dfcea3420209a3175d1c834b3d0d2dfef"},{"handle":"E5","slug":"weaver-onto-shed-rows","title":"Weaver runs each batch as a Shed row of the new kind and drops its own stop, re-entry, resume and lock code","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["weaver","shed"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":["F4"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nAbout 15–20% of Weaver's production code repeats in concept what Shed does: its stop and re-entry glue, part of its resume and its run lock.\nIt exists because Shed had no row that repeats or unfolds from a list.\n\n## Idea\n\nOnce Shed has that row kind and it has proven itself, move Weaver onto it:\neach batch becomes a pass or an unfolded row with Weaver as a thin producer,\nand Weaver's own stop record, re-entry, in-row resume and run lock give way to Shed's.\nThe model ladder, batch partitioning, verify fixer, in-flight strand probing and reset stay Weaver's, since Shed has no counterpart for them.\n\n## Bounds\n\n- Weaver's behaviour seen from the run stays the same: the same batches, ladder, journal, summary and outcome.\n- A bench against today's Weaver on the same plans checks it before Weaver's own loop is removed.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T11:46:46Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:46:48Z","design_file":"design-weaver-onto-shed-rows.md","decision_file":"","revision":"a3073a195e95a76a48b574317b2bc3fda01ae7b7c0cf75c67acec2a890c500c7"},{"handle":"E6","slug":"implementer-orientation-read","title":"An implementer batch gets the files and symbols its cards name, so it starts editing sooner","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["webster","weaver","stencil"],"status":"","project":"P8","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":["T9"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (`docs/research/webster/webster-time-2026-11-08.md`, lever 2)\n\nA Webster fork's first edit came a median 95 s in; orientation was 16.5% of fork time, 12.1 min per row.\n\n## Idea\n\nHand each batch the file and symbol list its cards name, or have it read them in one batched turn; the report assumed this halves orientation, 3 to 6 min per row.\nWeaver's cold batches are measured too before this is built.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T12:06:17Z","revised":"2026-11-28T12:06:18Z","design_file":"design-implementer-orientation-read.md","decision_file":"","revision":"133416bc4af64da67f5c6cadd778927804e59988a0ba918fbc946379f5851fbb"},{"handle":"E7","slug":"batch-gate-covers-cmd-lym","title":"The last batch gate covers cmd/lym and cross-package tests, so the plan-level verify seldom fails","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["gate","webster","weaver"],"status":"","project":"P8","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":["T9"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (`docs/research/webster/webster-time-2026-11-08.md`, lever 3)\n\n8 of 14 rows failed the plan-level verify on attempt 1, with `cmd/lym` in the failing ids of 5,\nand each paid a verify-fix session plus a second verify: 3.9 min per row, 6.8 per affected row.\n\n## Idea\n\nAdd `cmd/lym` to the batch gate's scope, or to the last batch's gate, so a batch session that can fix the failure meets it at once.\nThe cost of the added gate run is measured before this is built.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T12:06:19Z","revised":"2026-11-28T12:06:19Z","design_file":"design-batch-gate-covers-cmd-lym.md","decision_file":"","revision":"df7cfd53d643beac2cc830455152325ee7b27d810ca239d2c331b061b223646c"},{"handle":"E8","slug":"lock-holder-gates-first","title":"A gate run under the parent lock takes the next free gate slot ahead of runs that hold no lock","kind":"note","types":["enhancement"],"areas":["gate","landing"],"status":"","project":"P8","section":"Enhancements","waits_on":["T9"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (2026-11-28)\n\nA run gating tiers 0 to 2 under the parent lock waited for the same four gate slots as the runs queued behind its lock,\nwhose lock-free gates its landing would make stale anyway.\n`docs/research/webster/webster-time-2026-11-08.md` (lever 4) also measured 3.1 min of slot wait per Webster row, 27 min in one.\n\n## Idea\n\nGive gate slot requests a priority: a gate run under the parent lock first, then implementer gates, then the rest.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T12:06:20Z","revised":"2026-11-28T12:06:20Z","design_file":"design-lock-holder-gates-first.md","decision_file":"","revision":"f7d23ed40abeb15b45b8c414d7386130c6416e47fc7a809363f733afcea5f7db"},{"handle":"F1","slug":"autonomy-intake-runs","title":"Autonomy takes in new GitHub issues and runs them, so findings do not wait for the operator","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["board","orch","dobby"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Features","waits_on":["T38"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen\n\nRuns file their findings as GitHub issues on the hub repository, and runs and other agents file notes on the board, such as bugs.\nNobody takes either in unless the operator acts, so under autonomy they lie until morning.\nThe orch runs intake only when the operator asks (operator, 2026-11-28).\n\n## Operator decision (2026-11-28): autonomy comes in levels of scope\n\nWhat autonomy may start is a level of its own, beside how far it settles stops (project `self-healing-autonomy`):\n\n1. First level: autonomy acts only on tasks that are already tasks and already released.\n   It settles their stops and lands them, and starts no new work.\n2. Next level, a fuller autonomy: the orch also picks up the notes that runs and other agents file on the board, and incoming issues,\n   decides what to do with each, and may turn them into tasks and start them.\n\nTurning a note or issue into a task is the risky step, since autonomy could start large work the operator would not approve.\nRaddle splits are the example: their first draft is regularly reshaped by the operator, as project `self-healing-autonomy` went from eleven parts to five.\n\n## Idea\n\nAn autonomy module that owns the second level:\nwatch the board for new notes and the hub repository for new issues, import the issues, bundle the work by package,\nchain it against running and waiting tasks, start it as darn light, and report what it started.\nIt shares its home with the stop settling that project `self-healing-autonomy` builds.\n\n## Open\n\n- Whether this is lym code (dobby or a new module) or an orch procedure.\n- What the second level never starts on its own; likely raddle splits, projects, features, and darn solid or wide.\n- How the scope levels combine with `beam.autonomy`'s levels from `self-healing-autonomy`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-27T21:40:50Z","revised":"2026-11-28T10:11:27Z","design_file":"design-autonomy-intake-runs.md","decision_file":"","revision":"1f2769788edad8ff6431f4e8da3d64cff9dd386874d5f7fbdbea2fc6d9b19266"},{"handle":"F2","slug":"pipelined-chain-planning","title":"A dependent task plans ahead from its dependency's record and plan, and waits only to build","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["loom","batten","shed"],"status":"","project":"P5","section":"Features","waits_on":["T6"],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nA chain of tapestry tasks where B depends on A runs fully in series: B's Discussion and Plan wait until A has landed, though the plan writer is the slowest row.\nThe six serial parts of project `fabric-core` are the case at hand.\n\nIn Millhouse, which LoomYard grew from, the operator sped such chains up by hand, with success:\n\n- Once A's discussion record was approved, B's Discussion started, told to build on everything A had decided.\n- Plan writing for B likewise started before A was done, from A's record.\n- When A had landed, B merged A's changes, and a small fixer read the code changes and updated B's discussion and plan for what had moved.\n- Only then did B's builders start, so the builders were the only part that waited for A.\n\n## Design (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nOnly for the recipe that starts from a stored decision record: darn has no separate plan row to run ahead,\nand a run with its Discussion still to run never plans ahead, so B always plans from a record already approved.\n\n1. B depends on A.\n2. When A's plan is approved, B is released.\n3. B runs Discussion and Plan as usual, Plan Perch included, with A's record and plan as input.\n4. After B's plan is approved, a new producer row waits for B's dependencies: B parks there until A has landed.\n5. When A has landed, that row merges B's parent into B, bringing in A's change,\n   and a fixer updates B's decision record and plan for what A's change brought.\n   This stays small when A kept to its own plan.\n6. B then goes on to Webster as today.\n\nThe merge in step 5 also brings in whatever else landed on the parent, not only A.\nThat is no new cost: Parent-Align already meets the same landings later today.\nRunning the chain in a beam of its own keeps the parent quiet, so the merge in step 5 brings in little beside A.\n\n## Bounds\n\n- The choice is made up front, before B plans ahead, never after: a plan written ahead and then thrown away is the waste to avoid.\n  From A's record and plan, estimate how much of what B builds on A will change;\n  when A is estimated to change too much of it, B does not plan ahead and waits as today.\n- A run parked in the wait row of step 4 does nothing useful, so it must not hold one of `beam.max_runs`'s run places.\n- Each link planned ahead of landed code adds error to the plans, so the cascade is kept short.\n- Open: when B's plan counts as done for C to start.\n  Proposed: only when B has passed step 5, so C plans from a plan re-aligned against A's landed code and the chain runs at most one link ahead.\n- Not for now (operator, 2026-11-28).\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T11:14:18Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:54:11Z","design_file":"design-pipelined-chain-planning.md","decision_file":"","revision":"b4983820fc52c3089737a15d7f88e3e4079e42c883e6c0daa2f745686f121c71"},{"handle":"F3","slug":"plan-and-build-recipes","title":"Plan and build become recipes of their own, so a planned task waits for its dependencies with no run held","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["shed","loom","batten","board","dobby"],"status":"","project":"P5","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nMotif already runs Discussion as a recipe of its own: it runs, stores its record on the entry and lands.\nPlan and the implementer are rows inside tapestry, so a task planned ahead would have to park a run until its dependencies land.\n\n## Idea\n\nSplit tapestry into three recipes, each run as its own cycle:\n\n- X, motif: Discussion; stores the decision record in Bolt, since one record is often split into several tasks.\n- Y, plan: Plan and Plan Perch for one task.\n- Z, build: starts with a row that updates the plan for what landed since Y, then the implementer, review and landing.\n\nA full run is X, Y, Z. One X often gives several Y, and each Y gives exactly one Z.\n\n## Y and Z share one pair (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\n- When Y ends, its task turns into a Z task with the same slug, so Z gets the same task branch and the same weft branch.\n- The plan stays where it is written, in the pair's weft `_lym/plan`; it is never copied to Bolt, which would clutter it.\n- Batten skips Worktree-Teardown when a Y run ends, so the local pair stays standing until Z starts in it.\n- Nothing Z needs is trusted to origin: the pair is kept locally, since origin can be lost.\n- A Y run ends with no run held: Z waits through the dobby's existing dependency wait, with no parked run and no wait row.\n\n## Bounds\n\n- One entry carries the task through X, Y and Z, its stage shown on the board, so the operator follows one entry and not three.\n- Plan-ahead's bounds hold: Y of a dependent task runs only from an approved plan, and the choice to plan ahead is made up front.\n- A standing pair with no run holds disk only, never a run place.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T12:10:32Z","revised":"2026-11-28T12:18:09Z","design_file":"design-plan-and-build-recipes.md","decision_file":"","revision":"03310f9ad56f9c8b93ed63cc98ccf0ec226f5cd639546773824e2985d585e31a"},{"handle":"F4","slug":"shed-dynamic-rows","title":"Shed gains a row that repeats or unfolds into rows from a list, so a producer such as Weaver runs one step per batch","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["shed","weaver"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Seen (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\nWeaver runs its batches in its own Go loop inside tapestry's implementer row, because Shed could not step through them:\na recipe is a fixed file of rows and edges, and a producer's `Yield` ends the driver's loop (Weaver's decision record, Q1).\nAbout 15–20% of Weaver's production code, its stop and re-entry glue, part of its resume and its run lock, therefore repeats in concept what Shed does.\nReview rounds are a loop too, and the plan-ahead wait row of `pipelined-chain-planning` is a dynamic row.\n\n## Idea\n\nLet a recipe hold a row that repeats while its producer asks for another pass, or that unfolds into rows from a list its producer writes, such as a plan's batches.\nEach pass or unfolded row then gets Shed's own stops, re-entry, resume, history and arbiter, and Weaver shrinks to a thin producer per batch.\n\n## Bounds (operator, 2026-11-28)\n\n- Shed is a vital part of lym, and changing its logic this deep can have fatal consequences.\n- So the change is designed first, through motif, before anything is built,\n  and it only adds: every existing recipe, row and edge runs as today, and a recipe without the new row kind takes none of the new code paths.\n- Weaver stays as it is until the new row kind has proven itself; moving Weaver onto it is a task of its own.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T11:46:02Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:46:02Z","design_file":"design-shed-dynamic-rows.md","decision_file":"","revision":"b73f4ee71a7abec090720e9e7767051abb08ac365ad24a9d9ff44d02f6fc4a20"},{"handle":"F17","slug":"orchestrator-survey-ideas","title":"Remaining leads from the survey of comparable agent orchestrators (Oct 2026)","kind":"note","types":["feature"],"areas":["burler","shed","orch","quarry","report"],"status":"","project":"P2","section":"Features","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"Leads borrowed from comparable open-source agent orchestrators, surveyed in October 2026 (first imported from issue #671).\nNothing here is a commitment; these are leads to triage.\nThe orch checked each against the code on 2026-11-17: ideas lym has built or that proved moot are dropped,\npartly built ones name only what is still open, and the eight most worth doing are notes of their own in this project.\nEach bullet names its source project and the lym module it would touch.\n\n## Plan validation and concurrency (quarry, Plan-Write, board)\n\n- **Typed scope operations on cards** (foremerge): each scope carries an additive or breaking operation,\n  and deterministic rules flag colliding pairs even when files do not overlap.\n  Worth comparing against board labels and the alignment gate.\n- **Symbol leases between concurrent tasks** (Fusion, ORCH): expiring leases on symbol keys serialize overlapping tasks,\n  with a deterministic tie-break; lym keeps overlapping tasks apart by board labels.\n- **Commit-time reservation guard** (ntm): a pre-commit hook refuses a commit touching what another task holds; needs leases first.\n- **Criterion amendment ledger** (oh-my-claudecode): a card's acceptance text changes only through a recorded amendment in weft.\n\n## Review (burler, Bouncer)\n\n- **Quorum of differently-angled judges** (ccswarm): lym fans out lenses but has no quorum vote for high-risk segments.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's parent orch can be asked to see and veto each Code-Review round and each drift judge ruling\n  (`loom.code.review_ask_parent`, `drift.judge_ask_parent`); that is one extra judge, not a quorum.\n- **Risk tier by changed paths** (yylo): globs over sensitive paths choose the review depth and the gate scope.\n\n## Engine, recipes and governance (shed, tender, orch, board)\n\n- **Cross-run quarantine** (Bernstein): a task that fails three times across runs is held, unless the failures were the host's.\n- **Fork a run from a step with a tree snapshot** (Bernstein): `shed goto` moves the cursor but does not restore the warp tree.\n- **Warm retry guarded by a workspace hash** (Bernstein): resume the native session only when the tree is unchanged.\n  Partly met since `dfade5f9a`: a run agent whose session a crash, reboot or killed pane ended resumes it (engines with a `SessionResumer`, so not Codex);\n  no tree hash guards that resume, and a retry after a timeout or a finished run still starts fresh.\n  Since `f1311f21d` a row gate also replaces a cold gate fixer that a machine stop or a killed pane interrupted,\n  resuming its session where shuttle can; no tree hash guards that either.\n- **Evidence reuse by input identity** (yylo): verify is skipped on the same tree; a changed review prompt could rerun only the review.\n- **Guidance at the next step boundary** (Conductor): a queue of operator guidance for the next producer's prompt.\n  Partly met since 2026-11-19: `loom.notify_parent_on` mails the run's parent when a named row is reached,\n  so the parent can brief that row's agent as it starts; there is still no queue of guidance lym itself folds into the prompt.\n- **Batched operator questions with recorded assumptions** (Spec Kit `/clarify`, Conductor): Discussion-Write asks at most a few ranked questions,\n  each with a recommended answer, and records unanswered ones as assumptions.\n  Since 2026-11-19 a run's autonomous agents put their questions to the run's parent orch, not to the operator in their pane.\n  Discussion asks in its pane only when `loom.discussion.interactive` is `true`, which is off by default and forced off by autonomy mode (`30ed1ee58`),\n  so this lead concerns an interactive Discussion only.\n- **Rate-limit wait**: the orch watcher wakes the orch after a usage reset; tender itself schedules no resume.\n- **Standing goals and scheduled routines** (ORCH, CAO, Fusion): periodic triage, dependency bumps or `selfreport` sweeps.\n\n## Agent sessions (reed, shuttle)\n\n- **Waiting-for-input signal** (agent-of-empires): shuttle has an awaiting-input state; screen heuristics still decide readiness and startup.\n- **Reference status patterns and a paste pitfall** (CAO): compare its regexes and its tmux bracketed-paste note with shuttle's readiness and creel inject.\n- **Context forecasting** (ntm): the orch cycles its context; Webster's Master and the loom driver do not, and nothing forecasts.\n- **Staggered spawns** (ntm): space Webster forks and batten fan-out by learned rate limits.\n\n## Observability (report, weft)\n\n- **Hash-chained run journal** (Bernstein): a hash chain over producer outputs makes an archived run self-verifying.\n- **Unverified-completion rate** (Bernstein): a `lym report` view of completions with no test or gate behind them.\n- **Commit-to-record links** (AgentNote): weft links to warp by a `Warp-SHA:` trailer; a note or trailer on each warp commit could link back.\n- **Run diff** (ccswarm): `lym report` comparing two archived runs' timelines and token use.\n- **OpenTelemetry spans per producer** (Conductor).\n\n## Operator attention (orch, dobby, reed)\n\n- **Attention feed with acknowledgement states** (ntm): dobby's wait reminders have no seen, snoozed or acknowledged states.\n\n## Discussion review (Discussion-Write, stencils, pattern)\n\n- **Requirements-quality checklist** (Spec Kit `/checklist`): items with IDs the Discussion-review judge answers one by one.\n- **Fixed analysis categories** (Kiro): inconsistency, ambiguity, conflicts, unstated assumptions and missing edge cases as named lenses.\n- **PATTERN rules handed to reviewers** (Spec Kit constitution): its rules as numbered MUSTs in plan and code review prompts.\n- **Glob-scoped steering** (Kiro): stencils pull per-area guidance chosen by the paths of a plan's glyphs.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-10-16T06:52:07Z","revised":"2026-11-27T07:31:40Z","design_file":"design-orchestrator-survey-ideas.md","decision_file":"","revision":"45340d3f7ceab7c6c0ba773eca929ea01c976c2fb2ed6a0fb636c64ff4456713"},{"handle":"R1","slug":"landing-lock-effect-measure","title":"Measure how much landing-lock-only-for-land cut the wait for the parent lock","kind":"note","types":["research"],"areas":["landing","batten"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Research","waits_on":[],"brief":"","body":"## Why\n\n`landing-lock-only-for-land` (`d87a9ce24`) made Finalize merge and gate with no parent lock held, and take the lock only to land.\nWhether ready runs now land without queuing is unmeasured.\nOn 2026-11-28 `reset-partial-commit-push` still showed 38 minutes at Finalize with no agent activity before it took the lock on `main`.\nThe answer decides whether the landing queue note `landing-queue` is still worth building.\n\nUntil `finalize-owed-tips-gate` (`5c985d926`, 2026-11-28), a landing on a parent that was ahead of origin only by owed landing tips\nlost all of its `landing.finalize_lost_races` races and then merged and ran verify under the parent lock.\nFigures from landings before that commit can therefore include this fault.\n\n## What to measure\n\nFrom the drive reports and gate run logs of landings already made, no new runs:\n\n- For each landing, the time from Finalize's start to taking the parent lock, the time holding it, and the lost races (`landing.finalize_lost_races`).\n- How often a run fell back to merging and gating under the lock.\n- The same figures for the landings before `d87a9ce24`, for comparison.\n\nCap: the last 20 landings before `d87a9ce24` and the first 20 after `5c985d926`, on the prime of this hub.\nThe report lands as a commit under `docs/research/`.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-28T09:26:31Z","revised":"2026-11-28T11:03:45Z","design_file":"design-landing-lock-effect-measure.md","decision_file":"","revision":"3075fe0f180f02d42629cf344cf9d439b7f6784b274a2d404f19d72c86c4af30"},{"handle":"R4","slug":"antigravity-engine-spike","title":"Antigravity CLI engine spike","kind":"note","types":["research"],"areas":["shuttle"],"status":"","project":"P0","section":"Research","waits_on":[],"brief":"Hands-on spike of agy in a tmux pane: screens, hooks, transcript, login","body":"## Seen (orch, 2026-11-17)\n\nThe desk study `docs/research/gemini-antigravity-engine-study-2026-11-17.md` (`246c9146`) judged Antigravity CLI (`agy`) the better Google engine target.\nIts terms aim at third-party services reselling access, not at the operator driving the CLI with a tool they wrote, as with Codex and Claude (operator, 2026-11-17).\nSeveral of its readings are undocumented and can only be learnt hands-on.\n\n## Idea\n\nA hands-on spike like Codex's of 2026-10-24, run as darn docs, that installs `agy` and runs it in a tmux pane on Neve.\nIt answers the study's open points:\n\n- the busy, idle, trust and interrupt screens, and bracketed paste plus Enter through `paste-buffer -p`;\n- whether the keyring login works in a lym pane;\n- the hook payloads for SessionStart, PreToolUse, PostToolUse, PostInvocation and Stop (`continue`, `fullyIdle`);\n- the transcript's on-disk layout and token fields;\n- whether a conversation id can be chosen up front, and whether the `self` subagent inherits the caller's history.\n\nCap: one machine, one model, one short session per point, no lym code changed.\n","owner":"neve","recipe":"","created":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","revised":"2026-11-17T18:54:35Z","design_file":"design-antigravity-engine-spike.md","decision_file":"","revision":"050ddc1b61f6b055aa9d9b6c628533fe44d2d55b1714f47bce2bcd427506c7b5"}]};
